# System Context

This document gives the **mental model** of the project. For detailed flows, technology choices, and data stores see **architecture.mdc**. For strict per-service allowed/forbidden behavior see **service_boundaries.mdc**.

---

# Mental Model

**Blockchain → Event stream → Projections**

- Events originate on the blockchain (smart contract). Ingestion captures them and publishes to the AWS event bus.
- The event bus **fans out** so that both the Aggregator (AWS) and the Forwarder (AWS→GCP) receive every event — via SNS topic + two SQS queues (or two SQS queues from ingestion). See **architecture.mdc** §5.
- **AWS** holds the **operational projection** (DynamoDB, Aggregator); **GCP** holds the **analytical projection** (BigQuery, Analytics). Forwarder bridges events to GCP Pub/Sub.
- Services communicate **only via events**. No direct service-to-service HTTP/RPC. Each service owns its own data store.
- The **user interface** (e.g. Elixir/Phoenix LiveView in `user_interface/`) is a **client** of the Go services: it consumes their **gRPC read/query APIs** (Aggregator, optionally Analytics) and does not participate in the event bus.
- Delivery is **at-least-once**; all event consumers must be **idempotent**.
- **Voting window** (until when) and **voter eligibility** (who may vote) are enforced on the **smart contract**. Each proposal **freezes** its electorate at open; the global registry can change only for **future** proposals. Eligibility mutations are **transparent** via immutable events (Phase 3).

---

# Key Constraints

- **Event-driven only** — no synchronous cross-service calls.
- **Data ownership** — Aggregator owns DynamoDB; Analytics owns BigQuery; no cross-service DB access.
- **Idempotent consumers** — duplicate and delayed events must be tolerated.
- Changes to these principles require an **ADR** (Architecture Decision Record).

